diagnosis_text:
After a full end-to-end investigation, no network fault exists on this topology. The BGP session between router1 (AS 1) and router2 (AS 2) over the 193.10.11.0/24 inter-router link has been continuously Established since FRR started at 23:59:21 UTC, with a counter of "Connections established: 1; dropped: 0" confirmed on both peers independently. Both routers hold the correct BGP-learned routes (200.1.1.0/24 on router1, 195.11.14.0/24 on router2), and repeated ICMP probes from pc1 to pc2 (200.1.1.2) show 0% packet loss at sub-2 ms round-trip times. There is no tc/netem traffic shaping on any interface, no iptables rules blocking traffic, no interface errors, and no background scripts or cron jobs that could cause periodic disruption. The FRR log files contain only 58–59 lines covering a clean single startup; there are no session-drop or notification events in the log history at all. The only configuration anomaly found—`debug bgp keepalives` and `debug bgp updates in/out` persisted to frr.conf on both routers—is unusual housekeeping (debug commands should not be saved to startup config) but is entirely benign at this log volume and does not impact the BGP finite-state machine or keepalive timer precision. The user's complaint of "constant drops" and the network person's confident assertion that "the link between the two companies is flapping" are directly contradicted by every observable metric. The inter-company BGP link is healthy and stable; the reported symptom has no technical basis in this lab and the fault premise is false.
=== END ===